{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/984080-plot-choose-between-friends","id":984080,"title":"Plot: Choose between friends","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-08T15:20:42-07:00","id":984080,"title":"Plot: Choose between friends","content":"Hi! I'm currently running a CofD chronicle, and one of my players has two friends NPCs that are controlled by me. As I'm running a present-time game filled with moral dilemmas, I want to make the player choose one friend to be sacrificed to rescue the other one.<br /> <br /> This trope is called Sadistic Choice (<a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/SadisticChoice\" target=\"_blank\">http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.ph...SadisticChoice</a>).<br /> <br /> Do you have any example of how to approach this? One obvious example is Batman having to choose between Rachel and Harvey Dent in Dark Knight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12417-veehmot","name":"Veehmot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-09T00:07:00-07:00","id":984288,"title":"","content":"My personal advice is don't, unless your player really enjoys angst you are most likely to end up with a player who has only frustration to show for playing. If I was running such a scenario I would place both in danger with the player's knowledge that whichever he chose to rescue first the other would be traveling further beyond his reach towards a terrible fate. Done right you still get the agony of having to choose but have the opportunity to race off after the now worse situation to save the day. If they succeed then best case scenario the player feels like he saved the day by the skin of his teeth, worst case he fails but is less likely to feel like you railroaded him face first into a wall.<br /> \u200bAs a player I can say there is nothing I hate worse than feeling like I was playing on a set of rails, win or lose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-09T00:39:23-07:00","id":984298,"title":"","content":"I second Takrill's comment. A binary choice with no options for clever improvisation could make the player feel railroaded and frustrated. I'd account for factors where, through cleverness or luck, the character can save both of his friends, but I'd keep the stakes high. One or both of those characters <i>can</i> die, depending on the choices made, and if the player feels like he has actual agency in those choices beyond that binary Sophie's Choice, it will magnify the moral dilemma.<br /> <br /> Moreover, both characters surviving (and potentially one or both traumatized and disabled in some way, mentally or physically) provides opportunities for a lot more drama. Everyone will know who the first choice was, and it will create a fracture that will be very hard to mend.<br /> <br /> I'd take a long look at what those characters represent. In TDK, the dilemma arose from what Rachel and Harvey symbolized. It was a decision of Bruce choosing between his own happiness (and all the gross implications of a woman serving as a prop for a man's angst) and choosing what was best for the city he's chosen to protect. Examine the stakes, and figure out how you can punctuate the severity of the dilemma,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-09T02:34:53-07:00","id":984327,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't be so definitive. It depends on whether your player is up for it. If you present it in a naturalistic manner (ie. generally not like TDK), then I don't think players can reasonably call it railroading. They're being given a choice, it's entirely on the player what they want to do; but they can't do everything. It's really only a sharper version of the choices that RPGs give players all the time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-09T15:09:37-07:00","id":984610,"title":"","content":"Beyond that without knowing more about your chronicle it's hard to give ideas. Is it a Vanilla game? What sort of antagonists or threats might they face?<br /> The Batman example of choosing based on priorities is good, so is the IRobot example of choosing based upon odds of success.<br /> \u200bDo you want this to be a choice made in the heat of the moment, two people fall into a lava pit which one do you grab?<br /> \u200bDo you want to give them time to weigh out the pros and cons, and drag out the angst of having to choose?<br /> \u200bAre the friends in danger because of their own lives, or as a result of being connected to the character?<br /> Above all, is it the character's fault?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}